1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,640
Memories of my childhood live between the rings of sand around my ankles and the desert

2
00:00:06,640 --> 00:00:08,200
heat in my lungs.

3
00:00:08,200 --> 00:00:13,040
I still believe that nothing washes worry from tired skin better than the Nile and my

4
00:00:13,040 --> 00:00:15,000
grandma's hands.

5
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:19,200
Every day I go to school with the weight of dead neighbors on my shoulders.

6
00:00:19,200 --> 00:00:23,240
The first time I saw bomb smoke it didn't wind and billow like the heat from the kitchen

7
00:00:23,240 --> 00:00:27,880
hearth but it forced itself on the Darfur sky smothering the sun with tears that it stole

8
00:00:27,880 --> 00:00:29,520
from our bodies.

9
00:00:29,520 --> 00:00:33,360
The worst thing about genocide isn't the hunger, the murder, the politics, the government

10
00:00:33,360 --> 00:00:37,680
paid soldiers that chase you across borders and into camps.

11
00:00:37,680 --> 00:00:39,380
It's the silence.

12
00:00:39,380 --> 00:00:43,020
For three months they closed the schools down because people like us are an eyesore.

13
00:00:43,020 --> 00:00:45,160
So the first month we took it.

14
00:00:45,160 --> 00:00:46,620
The second we waited.

15
00:00:46,620 --> 00:00:49,820
The third month we met underneath the date palm trees drinking up every second our teachers

16
00:00:49,820 --> 00:00:52,280
gave us turning fruit pits into fractions.

17
00:00:52,280 --> 00:00:55,300
On the last day they came with a message, put them in their place.

18
00:00:55,300 --> 00:00:59,080
We didn't stand a chance, flesh was never meant to dance with several bullets so we prayed

19
00:00:59,080 --> 00:01:04,490
for the sun to come and melt daggers from our backs.

20
00:01:04,490 --> 00:01:07,330
I hid underneath the bed that day with four other people.

21
00:01:07,330 --> 00:01:11,830
Twelve years later and I can't help but wonder where my cousins hid when the soldiers torched

22
00:01:11,830 --> 00:01:15,550
the houses, through the bodies and the wells, so if the weapons didn't get you the poison

23
00:01:15,550 --> 00:01:19,550
would and sometimes they didn't want to use bullets because it would cost them more than

24
00:01:19,550 --> 00:01:20,550
we did.

25
00:01:20,550 --> 00:01:21,550
What does it matter?

26
00:01:21,550 --> 00:01:26,150
I've seen sixteen ways to stop a heart when you build nations on someone's bones.

27
00:01:26,150 --> 00:01:27,930
What sense does it make to break them?

28
00:01:27,930 --> 00:01:30,170
In one day my mother choked on rifle smoke.

29
00:01:30,170 --> 00:01:32,610
My father washed the blood from his face.

30
00:01:32,610 --> 00:01:37,310
My uncles carried half the bodies to the hospital and the rest to the grave and we watched.

31
00:01:37,310 --> 00:01:39,890
For every funeral we planned there were sixteen we couldn't.

32
00:01:39,890 --> 00:01:43,390
Half the sand in the Sahara tastes a lot like powdered bone.

33
00:01:43,390 --> 00:01:53,270
When the soldiers came, our blood on their ankles, I remember their laces, scarlet footprints

34
00:01:53,270 --> 00:01:54,270
on the floor.

35
00:01:54,270 --> 00:01:59,390
I remember waking to the sign of hatched voices in the night etched with the kind of sorrow

36
00:01:59,390 --> 00:02:02,430
that turns even the loudest dreams to ash.

37
00:02:02,430 --> 00:02:07,110
Our parents came home with broken collarbones and a taste of fear carved into their skin and

38
00:02:07,110 --> 00:02:09,590
it was impossible to believe in anything.

39
00:02:09,590 --> 00:02:15,010
Weather is the coldest thing in the desert and it burns you, bows you down to half your

40
00:02:15,010 --> 00:02:23,590
height and owns you and no one hears you because what could grow in the desert anyway?

41
00:02:23,590 --> 00:02:23,830
Thank you.

